ughly 38 AIRs each in segment 0, the GPU kernel time gets bottlenecked by a few large AIRs that can't run concurrently — they queue up instead, killing effective parallelism. I'm wondering if we could exploit this asymmetry differently: what if segment 1's GKR input eval could overlap with something from segment 0's processing? But segments run sequentially because each one depends on state from the previous segment. Though I should check whether the segments actually share state or if they're independent — if they're independent, there might be room to parallelize them.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":720,"cache_read_input_tokens":126091,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":720},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"50e92e47-0cd3-4f35-91c1-5f23da5f72d3","uuid":"edf6d270-d428-4255-b7a2-3bbe27d2b8ce"}
