{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01EbxCConTxaPyayES5rGRnR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JETsCtcvWA37TG4ken6dX9","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md","content":"# 2026-04-14-0030-rightsize-gkr-input-kernel-grid\n\nRight-size the GKR input evaluation kernel grid for small GLOBAL-mode AIRs. Currently, the CUDA launcher always uses `TASK_SIZE=65536` as the thread count for GLOBAL mode, launching 256 blocks regardless of the AIR's actual height. For the majority of AIRs at APC 300 (height << 65536), over 98% of threads exit immediately at the boundary check, yet each 256-block kernel saturates the RTX 4090's 128 SMs, preventing concurrent execution across the 8 multi-stream worker threads. Changing the launcher to use `min(TASK_SIZE, height)` allows small-AIR kernels to use proportionally fewer blocks (e.g., 4 blocks for height=1024 instead of 256), freeing SMs for concurrent kernel execution from other threads. This should dramatically improve multi-stream concurrency in GKR input eval — the single largest sub-component at APC 300 (seg0: 241ms, 17.6% of STARK excl trace).\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4263,"cache_read_input_tokens":171179,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4263},"output_tokens":40,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"50e92e47-0cd3-4f35-91c1-5f23da5f72d3","uuid":"d2db0a19-3a5f-446a-bf55-4aadeba41760"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JETsCtcvWA37TG4ken6dX9","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"50e92e47-0cd3-4f35-91c1-5f23da5f72d3","uuid":"6220607c-5883-453d-a2ec-bced0bb4d541","timestamp":"2026-04-13T23:20:05.287Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md","content":"# 2026-04-14-0030-rightsize-gkr-input-kernel-grid\n\nRight-size the GKR input evaluation kernel grid for small GLOBAL-mode AIRs. Currently, the CUDA launcher always uses `TASK_SIZE=65536` as the thread count for GLOBAL mode, launching 256 blocks regardless of the AIR's actual height. For the majority of AIRs at APC 300 (height << 65536), over 98% of threads exit immediately at the boundary check, yet each 256-block kernel saturates the RTX 4090's 128 SMs, preventing concurrent execution across the 8 multi-stream worker threads. Changing the launcher to use `min(TASK_SIZE, height)` allows small-AIR kernels to use proportionally fewer blocks (e.g., 4 blocks for height=1024 instead of 256), freeing SMs for concurrent kernel execution from other threads. This should dramatically improve multi-stream concurrency in GKR input eval — the single largest sub-component at APC 300 (seg0: 241ms, 17.6% of STARK excl trace).\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":3,"newStart":1,"newLines":3,"lines":["-# 2026-04-13-2200-prealloc-round0-threshold-buffers","+# 2026-04-14-0030-rightsize-gkr-input-kernel-grid"," ","-Pre-allocate per-thread reusable GPU buffers for Round 0 constraint and interaction evaluation using an adaptive size threshold, rather than pre-allocating for the maximum AIR size. The previous pre-allocation attempt (prealloc-round0-buffers) failed because it pre-allocated for the largest AIR's intermediates (~1GB per thread × 8 threads = 8GB), causing systemic GPU memory pressure that degraded all phases. The adaptive approach pre-allocates buffers sized for the Nth-percentile AIR (e.g., 95th percentile), covering 95%+ of AIRs while keeping total allocation manageable. Small/medium AIRs (the vast majority at APC 300) skip the global MEMORY_MANAGER mutex entirely, while the few large AIRs fall back to dynamic allocation. This addresses the same root cause as the successful GKR pre-alloc optimization (which saved 217ms): eliminating per-AIR mutex contention and cudaMallocAsync/cudaFreeAsync serialization across 8 concurrent worker threads.","+Right-size the GKR input evaluation kernel grid for small GLOBAL-mode AIRs. Currently, the CUDA launcher always uses `TASK_SIZE=65536` as the thread count for GLOBAL mode, launching 256 blocks regardless of the AIR's actual height. For the majority of AIRs at APC 300 (height << 65536), over 98% of threads exit immediately at the boundary check, yet each 256-block kernel saturates the RTX 4090's 128 SMs, preventing concurrent execution across the 8 multi-stream worker threads. Changing the launcher to use `min(TASK_SIZE, height)` allows small-AIR kernels to use proportionally fewer blocks (e.g., 4 blocks for height=1024 instead of 256), freeing SMs for concurrent kernel execution from other threads. This should dramatically improve multi-stream concurrency in GKR input eval — the single largest sub-component at APC 300 (seg0: 241ms, 17.6% of STARK excl trace)."]}],"originalFile":"# 2026-04-13-2200-prealloc-round0-threshold-buffers\n\nPre-allocate per-thread reusable GPU buffers for Round 0 constraint and interaction evaluation using an adaptive size threshold, rather than pre-allocating for the maximum AIR size. The previous pre-allocation attempt (prealloc-round0-buffers) failed because it pre-allocated for the largest AIR's intermediates (~1GB per thread × 8 threads = 8GB), causing systemic GPU memory pressure that degraded all phases. The adaptive approach pre-allocates buffers sized for the Nth-percentile AIR (e.g., 95th percentile), covering 95%+ of AIRs while keeping total allocation manageable. Small/medium AIRs (the vast majority at APC 300) skip the global MEMORY_MANAGER mutex entirely, while the few large AIRs fall back to dynamic allocation. This addresses the same root cause as the successful GKR pre-alloc optimization (which saved 217ms): eliminating per-AIR mutex contention and cudaMallocAsync/cudaFreeAsync serialization across 8 concurrent worker threads.\n"}}
