{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VWViHmyTJHxU7QSXkQ2edR","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: Right-size GKR input eval kernel grid for small AIRs\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tReduce GKR input evaluation wall time at APC 300 by fixing a grid-sizing inefficiency: the CUDA launcher for `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel<GLOBAL=true>` always launches `TASK_SIZE/256 = 256` blocks regardless of the AIR's actual `permutation_height`. For the majority of AIRs at APC 300 where height << 65536, over 98% of launched threads exit at the `if (row < permutation_height)` boundary check, but the 256-block kernel still saturates all 128 SMs on the RTX 4090, preventing concurrent kernel execution from the 8 multi-stream worker threads. The fix uses `min(TASK_SIZE, permutation_height)` as the thread count, shrinking the grid proportionally for small AIRs and freeing SMs for inter-stream concurrency.\n6\t\n7\t## Current Code Path\n8\t\n9\t### Entry point\n10\t`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs:215` — `log_gkr_input_evals()`\n11\t\n12\t1. Allocates `leaves` buffer for all interactions: `DeviceBuffer<Frac<EF>>::with_capacity(total_leaves)` (line 228)\n13\t2. Builds work items sorted by descending height (lines 232-252)\n14\t3. Pre-computes max buffer sizes, pre-allocates per-thread `GkrThreadBuffers` (lines 254-321)\n15\t4. Dispatches to 8 OS threads (when ≥100 AIRs). Each thread iterates its work items calling `process_gkr_input_air()` (lines 328-348)\n16\t\n17\t### Per-AIR processing\n18\t`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs:112` — `process_gkr_input_air()`\n19\t\n20\t1. Copies `partition_ptrs` and optionally `public_values` to pre-allocated device buffers (lines 141-156)\n21\t2. Computes `is_global = buffer_size > 10` (line 160)\n22\t3. Computes `num_rows_per_tile = height.div_ceil(TASK_SIZE).max(1)` (line 162)\n23\t4. Calls `logup_gkr_input_eval(is_global, ..., height, num_rows_per_tile)` (line 174)\n24\t\n25\t### CUDA launcher\n26\t`crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.cu:216` — `_logup_gkr_input_eval()`\n27\t\n28\t```cpp\n29\tauto count = is_global ? TASK_SIZE : permutation_height;  // BUG: always 65536 for GLOBAL\n30\tauto [grid, block] = kernel_launch_params(count, 256);    // grid = 256 blocks\n31\t```\n32\t\n33\tFor GLOBAL mode: always `grid = {256, 1, 1}`, `block = {256, 1, 1}` = 65536 threads. For a height=1024 AIR, threads 1024-65535 do nothing but still occupy SMs.\n34\t\n35\t### Kernel\n36\t`crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.cu:66` — `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel<GLOBAL>()`\n37\t\n38\t```cpp\n39\tuint32_t task_offset = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;\n40\tuint32_t task_stride = gridDim.x * blockDim.x;\n41\tFpExt *intermediates_ptr = (FpExt *)d_intermediates + task_offset;  // GLOBAL\n42\tuint32_t intermediate_stride = task_stride;                          // GLOBAL\n43\tfor (uint32_t j = 0; j < num_rows_per_tile; j++) {\n44\t    uint32_t row = task_offset + j * task_stride;\n45\t    if (row < permutation_height) { /* evaluate DAG */ }\n46\t}\n47\t```\n48\t\n49\tThe intermediates buffer is indexed as `intermediates_ptr + node_idx * intermediate_stride`. With reduced grid, `intermediate_stride = min(TASK_SIZE, height)`, and the buffer access stays within `height * buffer_size` elements ≤ the pre-allocated `TASK_SIZE * max_buffer_size`.\n50\t\n51\t### Why current code is slow\n52\t\n53\tnsys profiling shows `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel<true>` has 486 instances totaling 456ms GPU time. Each GLOBAL kernel launches 256 blocks. On the RTX 4090 with 128 SMs and heavy register pressure (~2 blocks/SM occupancy), a single GLOBAL kernel fills all SMs. When 8 threads each launch GLOBAL kernels concurrently, the GPU serializes them — measured concurrency for seg0 is only 1.37x despite 8 streams. Per-segment metrics confirm: seg0 GKR input eval = 241ms, seg1 = 62ms. Seg0 is the single largest sub-component of STARK excl trace.\n54\t\n55\t## Changes\n56\t\n57\t### Change 1: CUDA launcher grid sizing (primary fix)\n58\t\n59\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.cu`, line 231\n60\t\n61\t**What**: Change the `count` computation for GLOBAL mode from `TASK_SIZE` to `min(TASK_SIZE, permutation_height)`.\n62\t\n63\t**Before**:\n64\t```cpp\n65\tauto count = is_global ? TASK_SIZE : permutation_height;\n66\t```\n67\t\n68\t**After**:\n69\t```cpp\n70\tauto count = is_global ? min((uint32_t)TASK_SIZE, permutation_height) : permutation_height;\n71\t```\n72\t\n73\t**Why**: For height=1024, this changes the grid from 256 blocks to 4 blocks. The 4 blocks occupy 4 SMs instead of 128, freeing 124 SMs for concurrent kernels from other streams. No computation changes — the kernel's loop body is identical, and idle threads (which did no work anyway) are simply not launched.\n74\t\n75\t**Correctness**: The kernel accesses `intermediates[task_offset + node_idx * task_stride]` where `task_stride = gridDim.x * blockDim.x = count`. For small AIRs, `count = height`, so the maximum intermediates index is `(height - 1) + (buffer_size - 1) * height = height * buffer_size - 1`. The pre-allocated buffer has capacity `TASK_SIZE * max_buffer_size ≥ height * buffer_size`. All accesses are in bounds.\n76\t\n77\t### Change 2: Tighten Rust-side intermediates pre-allocation (secondary)\n78\t\n79\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`, lines 260-265\n80\t\n81\t**What**: Compute `max_intermediates_len` using `min(TASK_SIZE, height) * buffer_size` instead of `TASK_SIZE * buffer_size`.\n82\t\n83\t**Before**:\n84\t```rust\n85\tif is_global {\n86\t    max_intermediates_len = max_intermediates_len.max(TASK_SIZE as usize * buffer_size);\n87\t}\n88\t```\n89\t\n90\t**After**:\n91\t```rust\n92\tif is_global {\n93\t    let actual_task_count = (TASK_SIZE as usize).min(height);\n94\t    max_intermediates_len = max_intermediates_len.max(actual_task_count * buffer_size);\n95\t}\n96\t```\n97\t\n98\t**Why**: Matches the pre-allocation to the actual kernel usage after Change 1. For APC 300, the largest AIR still uses full TASK_SIZE (height ≥ 65536), so this doesn't reduce the max for that case. But it's a correctness alignment that would matter if all AIRs had height < TASK_SIZE (e.g., different workloads). Low risk, ensures the pre-allocation assumption matches kernel behavior.\n99\t\n100\t## Invariants\n101\t\n102\t1. **Correctness**: The kernel computation is unchanged. All threads that previously did useful work still do the same work. The only change is that idle threads (row ≥ permutation_height) are not launched.\n103\t2. **Intermediates buffer bounds**: For any height ≤ TASK_SIZE, `height * buffer_size ≤ TASK_SIZE * max_buffer_size` (the pre-allocated capacity). For height > TASK_SIZE, the grid is unchanged (count = TASK_SIZE).\n104\t3. **num_rows_per_tile**: This is computed on the Rust side as `height.div_ceil(TASK_SIZE).max(1)`. For height ≤ TASK_SIZE: `num_rows_per_tile = 1`, which is correct — each thread processes at most 1 row. For height > TASK_SIZE: unchanged.\n105\t4. **APC 0 no-regression**: At APC 0, there are only 99 AIRs (below the 100-AIR multi-threading threshold). Processing is single-threaded, so concurrency improvements don't apply. Large AIRs at APC 0 already use height ≥ TASK_SIZE, so no grid change. No regression expected.\n106\t5. **Output layout**: The `d_fracs` output pointer is per-AIR and writes to the correct offset in the shared `leaves` buffer. No change to output addressing.\n107\t\n108\t## Measurement Plan\n109\t\n110\t### Commands\n111\t```bash\n112\t# In powdr repo:\n113\tPROVE_BIN=\"$(cargo metadata --format-version 1 --no-deps 2>/dev/null | python3 -c 'import sys,json; print(json.load(sys.stdin)[\"target_directory\"])')/release/powdr_openvm_riscv\"\n114\t\n115\t# Build (includes CUDA rebuild)\n116\tcargo build --bin powdr_openvm_riscv -r --features \"metrics,cuda\"\n117\t\n118\t# Run benchmarks (3 runs each for stability)\n119\tfor i in 1 2 3; do\n120\t  $PROVE_BIN prove --artifact results/pairing/apc300.cbor --input 0 --metrics results/pairing/apc300/metrics_after_$i.json --recursion\n121\t  $PROVE_BIN prove --artifact results/pairing/apc000.cbor --input 0 --metrics results/pairing/apc000/metrics_after_$i.json --recursion\n122\tdone\n123\t\n124\t# Profile with nsys\n125\tnsys profile --output results/pairing/nsys_after_apc300 --force-overwrite true --trace cuda,nvtx,osrt --sample none --stats true -- $PROVE_BIN prove --artifact results/pairing/apc300.cbor --input 0 --recursion\n126\t```\n127\t\n128\t### Expected results\n129\t- **GKR input eval seg0**: 241ms → 110-160ms (1.5-2.2x improvement)\n130\t- **GKR input eval seg1**: 62ms → 40-55ms\n131\t- **STARK excl trace APC 300**: 1372ms → 1220-1300ms (5-11% improvement)\n132\t- **APC 0 no-regression**: STARK excl trace ≈ 2131ms ± 20ms\n133\t- **nsys verification**: Total blocks for `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel<true>` should decrease significantly (from ~124K to ~20-40K total across all phases)\n134\t\n135\t### Key metrics to compare\n136\t- `prover.rap_constraints.logup_gkr.input_evals_time_ms` per segment\n137\t- `prover.rap_constraints.logup_gkr_time_ms` per segment\n138\t- Overall `STARK (excl. trace)` via spec.py\n139\t\n140\t## Rollback Criteria\n141\t\n142\t1. **STARK excl trace APC 300 improvement < 30ms** (median of 3 runs): The fix targets a 100-150ms improvement. If the improvement is under 30ms, either the analysis was wrong about GPU saturation being the bottleneck, or other serialization points dominate.\n143\t2. **APC 0 regression > 20ms**: Any regression at APC 0 indicates a correctness or performance problem with the grid change for large AIRs.\n144\t3. **Correctness failure**: Any verification error or incorrect proof output at any APC configuration.\n145\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"50e92e47-0cd3-4f35-91c1-5f23da5f72d3","uuid":"7851f949-dffc-419f-b4a7-4676f4f29734","timestamp":"2026-04-13T23:24:07.815Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-14-0030-rightsize-gkr-input-kernel-grid/plan.md","content":"# Plan: Right-size GKR input eval kernel grid for small AIRs\n\n## Goal\n\nReduce GKR input evaluation wall time at APC 300 by fixing a grid-sizing inefficiency: the CUDA launcher for `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel<GLOBAL=true>` always launches `TASK_SIZE/256 = 256` blocks regardless of the AIR's actual `permutation_height`. For the majority of AIRs at APC 300 where height << 65536, over 98% of launched threads exit at the `if (row < permutation_height)` boundary check, but the 256-block kernel still saturates all 128 SMs on the RTX 4090, preventing concurrent kernel execution from the 8 multi-stream worker threads. The fix uses `min(TASK_SIZE, permutation_height)` as the thread count, shrinking the grid proportionally for small AIRs and freeing SMs for inter-stream concurrency.\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n### Entry point\n`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs:215` — `log_gkr_input_evals()`\n\n1. Allocates `leaves` buffer for all interactions: `DeviceBuffer<Frac<EF>>::with_capacity(total_leaves)` (line 228)\n2. Builds work items sorted by descending height (lines 232-252)\n3. Pre-computes max buffer sizes, pre-allocates per-thread `GkrThreadBuffers` (lines 254-321)\n4. Dispatches to 8 OS threads (when ≥100 AIRs). Each thread iterates its work items calling `process_gkr_input_air()` (lines 328-348)\n\n### Per-AIR processing\n`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs:112` — `process_gkr_input_air()`\n\n1. Copies `partition_ptrs` and optionally `public_values` to pre-allocated device buffers (lines 141-156)\n2. Computes `is_global = buffer_size > 10` (line 160)\n3. Computes `num_rows_per_tile = height.div_ceil(TASK_SIZE).max(1)` (line 162)\n4. Calls `logup_gkr_input_eval(is_global, ..., height, num_rows_per_tile)` (line 174)\n\n### CUDA launcher\n`crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.cu:216` — `_logup_gkr_input_eval()`\n\n```cpp\nauto count = is_global ? TASK_SIZE : permutation_height;  // BUG: always 65536 for GLOBAL\nauto [grid, block] = kernel_launch_params(count, 256);    // grid = 256 blocks\n```\n\nFor GLOBAL mode: always `grid = {256, 1, 1}`, `block = {256, 1, 1}` = 65536 threads. For a height=1024 AIR, threads 1024-65535 do nothing but still occupy SMs.\n\n### Kernel\n`crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.cu:66` — `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel<GLOBAL>()`\n\n```cpp\nuint32_t task_offset = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;\nuint32_t task_stride = gridDim.x * blockDim.x;\nFpExt *intermediates_ptr = (FpExt *)d_intermediates + task_offset;  // GLOBAL\nuint32_t intermediate_stride = task_stride;                          // GLOBAL\nfor (uint32_t j = 0; j < num_rows_per_tile; j++) {\n    uint32_t row = task_offset + j * task_stride;\n    if (row < permutation_height) { /* evaluate DAG */ }\n}\n```\n\nThe intermediates buffer is indexed as `intermediates_ptr + node_idx * intermediate_stride`. With reduced grid, `intermediate_stride = min(TASK_SIZE, height)`, and the buffer access stays within `height * buffer_size` elements ≤ the pre-allocated `TASK_SIZE * max_buffer_size`.\n\n### Why current code is slow\n\nnsys profiling shows `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel<true>` has 486 instances totaling 456ms GPU time. Each GLOBAL kernel launches 256 blocks. On the RTX 4090 with 128 SMs and heavy register pressure (~2 blocks/SM occupancy), a single GLOBAL kernel fills all SMs. When 8 threads each launch GLOBAL kernels concurrently, the GPU serializes them — measured concurrency for seg0 is only 1.37x despite 8 streams. Per-segment metrics confirm: seg0 GKR input eval = 241ms, seg1 = 62ms. Seg0 is the single largest sub-component of STARK excl trace.\n\n## Changes\n\n### Change 1: CUDA launcher grid sizing (primary fix)\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.cu`, line 231\n\n**What**: Change the `count` computation for GLOBAL mode from `TASK_SIZE` to `min(TASK_SIZE, permutation_height)`.\n\n**Before**:\n```cpp\nauto count = is_global ? TASK_SIZE : permutation_height;\n```\n\n**After**:\n```cpp\nauto count = is_global ? min((uint32_t)TASK_SIZE, permutation_height) : permutation_height;\n```\n\n**Why**: For height=1024, this changes the grid from 256 blocks to 4 blocks. The 4 blocks occupy 4 SMs instead of 128, freeing 124 SMs for concurrent kernels from other streams. No computation changes — the kernel's loop body is identical, and idle threads (which did no work anyway) are simply not launched.\n\n**Correctness**: The kernel accesses `intermediates[task_offset + node_idx * task_stride]` where `task_stride = gridDim.x * blockDim.x = count`. For small AIRs, `count = height`, so the maximum intermediates index is `(height - 1) + (buffer_size - 1) * height = height * buffer_size - 1`. The pre-allocated buffer has capacity `TASK_SIZE * max_buffer_size ≥ height * buffer_size`. All accesses are in bounds.\n\n### Change 2: Tighten Rust-side intermediates pre-allocation (secondary)\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`, lines 260-265\n\n**What**: Compute `max_intermediates_len` using `min(TASK_SIZE, height) * buffer_size` instead of `TASK_SIZE * buffer_size`.\n\n**Before**:\n```rust\nif is_global {\n    max_intermediates_len = max_intermediates_len.max(TASK_SIZE as usize * buffer_size);\n}\n```\n\n**After**:\n```rust\nif is_global {\n    let actual_task_count = (TASK_SIZE as usize).min(height);\n    max_intermediates_len = max_intermediates_len.max(actual_task_count * buffer_size);\n}\n```\n\n**Why**: Matches the pre-allocation to the actual kernel usage after Change 1. For APC 300, the largest AIR still uses full TASK_SIZE (height ≥ 65536), so this doesn't reduce the max for that case. But it's a correctness alignment that would matter if all AIRs had height < TASK_SIZE (e.g., different workloads). Low risk, ensures the pre-allocation assumption matches kernel behavior.\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Correctness**: The kernel computation is unchanged. All threads that previously did useful work still do the same work. The only change is that idle threads (row ≥ permutation_height) are not launched.\n2. **Intermediates buffer bounds**: For any height ≤ TASK_SIZE, `height * buffer_size ≤ TASK_SIZE * max_buffer_size` (the pre-allocated capacity). For height > TASK_SIZE, the grid is unchanged (count = TASK_SIZE).\n3. **num_rows_per_tile**: This is computed on the Rust side as `height.div_ceil(TASK_SIZE).max(1)`. For height ≤ TASK_SIZE: `num_rows_per_tile = 1`, which is correct — each thread processes at most 1 row. For height > TASK_SIZE: unchanged.\n4. **APC 0 no-regression**: At APC 0, there are only 99 AIRs (below the 100-AIR multi-threading threshold). Processing is single-threaded, so concurrency improvements don't apply. Large AIRs at APC 0 already use height ≥ TASK_SIZE, so no grid change. No regression expected.\n5. **Output layout**: The `d_fracs` output pointer is per-AIR and writes to the correct offset in the shared `leaves` buffer. No change to output addressing.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\n### Commands\n```bash\n# In powdr repo:\nPROVE_BIN=\"$(cargo metadata --format-version 1 --no-deps 2>/dev/null | python3 -c 'import sys,json; print(json.load(sys.stdin)[\"target_directory\"])')/release/powdr_openvm_riscv\"\n\n# Build (includes CUDA rebuild)\ncargo build --bin powdr_openvm_riscv -r --features \"metrics,cuda\"\n\n# Run benchmarks (3 runs each for stability)\nfor i in 1 2 3; do\n  $PROVE_BIN prove --artifact results/pairing/apc300.cbor --input 0 --metrics results/pairing/apc300/metrics_after_$i.json --recursion\n  $PROVE_BIN prove --artifact results/pairing/apc000.cbor --input 0 --metrics results/pairing/apc000/metrics_after_$i.json --recursion\ndone\n\n# Profile with nsys\nnsys profile --output results/pairing/nsys_after_apc300 --force-overwrite true --trace cuda,nvtx,osrt --sample none --stats true -- $PROVE_BIN prove --artifact results/pairing/apc300.cbor --input 0 --recursion\n```\n\n### Expected results\n- **GKR input eval seg0**: 241ms → 110-160ms (1.5-2.2x improvement)\n- **GKR input eval seg1**: 62ms → 40-55ms\n- **STARK excl trace APC 300**: 1372ms → 1220-1300ms (5-11% improvement)\n- **APC 0 no-regression**: STARK excl trace ≈ 2131ms ± 20ms\n- **nsys verification**: Total blocks for `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel<true>` should decrease significantly (from ~124K to ~20-40K total across all phases)\n\n### Key metrics to compare\n- `prover.rap_constraints.logup_gkr.input_evals_time_ms` per segment\n- `prover.rap_constraints.logup_gkr_time_ms` per segment\n- Overall `STARK (excl. trace)` via spec.py\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\n1. **STARK excl trace APC 300 improvement < 30ms** (median of 3 runs): The fix targets a 100-150ms improvement. If the improvement is under 30ms, either the analysis was wrong about GPU saturation being the bottleneck, or other serialization points dominate.\n2. **APC 0 regression > 20ms**: Any regression at APC 0 indicates a correctness or performance problem with the grid change for large AIRs.\n3. **Correctness failure**: Any verification error or incorrect proof output at any APC configuration.\n","numLines":145,"startLine":1,"totalLines":145}}}
